Here's the standard approach:

### 1. Define theme resource dictionaries

Create three XAML files (e.g., `Resources/Styles/`):

**LightTheme.xaml**
```xml
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
                    x:Class="MyApp.Resources.Styles.LightTheme">
    <Color x:Key="PageBackground">#FFFFFF</Color>
    <Color x:Key="PrimaryText">#1A1A1A</Color>
    <Color x:Key="Accent">#0078D4</Color>
</ResourceDictionary>
```

**DarkTheme.xaml** and **HighContrastTheme.xaml** — same keys, different values.

### 2. Apply one theme at startup in `App.xaml.cs`

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    public App()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        ApplyTheme(Preferences.Get("AppTheme", "Light"));
    }

    public static void ApplyTheme(string themeName)
    {
        var mergedDictionaries = Current!.Resources.MergedDictionaries;
        mergedDictionaries.Clear();

        var theme = themeName switch
        {
            "Dark"         => new DarkTheme(),
            "HighContrast" => new HighContrastTheme(),
            _              => new LightTheme(),
        };

        mergedDictionaries.Add(theme);
        Preferences.Set("AppTheme", themeName);
    }
}
```

### 3. Use the keys in your pages/styles

```xml
<ContentPage BackgroundColor="{StaticResource PageBackground}">
    <Label TextColor="{DynamicResource PrimaryText}" Text="Hello" />
</ContentPage>
```

> **Key point:** Use `DynamicResource` (not `StaticResource`) for any property that should update live when the theme switches.

### 4. Settings page picker

```xml
<Picker x:Name="ThemePicker"
        Title="Theme"
        SelectedIndexChanged="OnThemeChanged">
    <Picker.ItemsSource>
        <x:Array Type="{x:Type x:String}">
            <x:String>Light</x:String>
            <x:String>Dark</x:String>
            <x:String>HighContrast</x:String>
        </x:Array>
    </Picker.ItemsSource>
</Picker>
```

```csharp
void OnThemeChanged(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    if (ThemePicker.SelectedItem is string theme)
        App.ApplyTheme(theme);
}
```

**Summary of the pattern:**
- One `ResourceDictionary` per theme, all sharing the same keys.
- `ApplyTheme()` clears and re-adds the active dictionary.
- `DynamicResource` bindings pick up changes immediately.
- `Preferences` persists the choice across launches.
